> On 2007-11-19, Derek Ray <derek@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org> wrote:
>> Actually, I have spent some time considering this due to the Yeeny-bones
>> Spork thing.  It's only happened on one occurrence in 3400+ games, so it's
>> certainly not going to be common, but I'm rather fond of the idea at the
>> moment that a major demon would just pick up all the loot from the bones 
>> and go into "meditative" mode; ie. sleep until he actually sees you and
>> you come close to him.  That, or outright turn peaceful, which is a
>> little weird but wouldn't be so bad either.
>
> Why not have the Demon Lord claim the soul of the hapless adventurer
> who summoned him and return to Hell with his prize.  Possibly taking
> any nifty toys the adventurer might have been carrying with him when
> he goes.  It removes the excessive threat (and reward), and as a bonus
> it makes more "story" sense than having him peaceful.

This could actually be written "just don't leave bones." -- with no
thing-that-killed-you and no loot to pick up, why bother writing a bones
file?

I'd rather leave in the potential threat... just not make it "take two
steps and die" sort of thing in most cases.

> The meditation idea doesn't actually remove the out-of-depth threat,
> as the demon could be next to the stairs and the player's pet could
> wake him before the player has time to stop it.  

A demon directly next to the stairs starts to fall into "intentional" 
territory; fortunately it is too hard to actually do this in a short
enough period of time to worry about.  Plus, I like having the loot
still available... you just have to beat up a major demon to get it.

> You may need to add boulder-blocked stairs to make this plausible,
> but it could be constructed with a no-E demon.

Right.  But not constructed in a short period of time (see other thread
for why I'm concerned about 'time', not 'difficulty'; with the random
chance of bones even being left in the first place, something that
takes an hour or longer to set up for only a fractional chance
of leaving bones will never be an issue.  Something that can be done in
five or ten minutes will be much more of one.)

> A completely different suggestion: you could tie E's effectiveness to
> either Dlvl (or max Dlvl) or XP-lvl rather than/in addition to monster
> level.  

I thought about both of these, and was concerned that this would simply
lead to players trying to artificially reduce their XL.  DL seems good
but I'd rather have the danger tied into the critter you're facing --
ie. stronger monsters are stronger for a number of reasons, not just an
arbitrary difficulty number.  This should be true no matter where you
are in the dungeon.

> The point is, rather than your "tougher monsters ignore the word", a
> different design principle would be "tougher characters don't need the
> word".  

Which is currently true in practice in Spork; 78 players have ascended
out of 3,491 games just on my server alone, a 2.2% ascension ratio.
(For comparison, NAO's ratio is roughly 1%.)  That's _with_ the Elbereth
change as described in since day 1.

